# Web colours

Use these colours on web pages and Home Assistant dashboards. Use a functional colour for its role first, such as the brand, link or error colour. Use the wider palette only for supporting elements such as illustrations or custom cards.

Every value on this page is the GOV.UK web palette, unchanged.

<%= colour_copy_status %>

## Web palette

Use these colours for supporting elements, such as illustrations, or in custom components where it is appropriate.

In GOV.UK Frontend, call the `govuk-colour` Sass function instead of typing a value. For example, `govuk-colour("blue")` gives Primary blue. Add `$variant` for a tint or shade, such as `govuk-colour("red", $variant: "tint-25")`.

Most colours have tints at 25%, 50%, 80% and 95%, and shades at 25% and 50%. Black has tints for greys. White has no variants.

<%= colour_palette('web') %>

## Functional colours

A functional colour has one job. Use it only for that job, and use the palette for anything else.

- Use the `govuk-functional-colour` Sass function where it is available, for example `govuk-functional-colour("brand")` instead of `#1D70B8`.
- Use `inverse-text` for text on dark backgrounds.
- Use `template-background` for the page behind the content and `body-background` for the content area.
- Use `focus` only for the element that has keyboard focus.
- Use `error` only for error messages and `success` only for success messages.
- Use `hover` only for the hover state of inputs.

Where the default civica theme (Blue), in light mode, sets a Home Assistant custom property to exactly this value, the property is listed. Other theme colours and dark mode use different values, so a listed property does not always give this colour.

